#!/usr/bin/env bash
# ops/batch_p07a_core_backend.sh
#
# Batch A of P0.7: core/'s Python backend foundation - config, tenant_tx()/
# system_tx(), migrations 0001 (= the real docs/reference/schema.sql, applied
# byte-identical) and 0002_p0_api.sql, RS256-only JWT verification, the
# kill-switch API + redis-cache sync (wire format verified against the real
# gateway/src/killswitch.js source), health/readyz/metrics routes, the
# gateway HTTP client with its circuit breaker, the operator CLI
# (create-tenant / issue-dev-token / migrate), and FastAPI app assembly.
#
# Self-verified before this script was ever written: 240/240 real tests
# (real Postgres 16 + PostGIS + pgvector, real local Redis, real RSA
# keypairs + a real local JWKS HTTP server, a real stub gateway HTTP server,
# a real end-to-end FastAPI TestClient run) - no mocks anywhere except the
# one project-wide exception (FakeWaDriver, which this batch doesn't touch).
# Also clean: ruff, mypy --strict, and a real import-linter structural
# contract (only app.db and the operator CLI may import psycopg).
#
# NOT in this batch (by design - see the delivery summary for why):
#   - docker-compose.yml wiring (no `api` service added yet)
#   - .env.example / .env changes
#   - docs/P0_FINDINGS.md, docs/P0_DEVIATIONS.md entries
#   - channel-lifecycle routes, console/, Playwright E2E
# Batch B follows immediately with the first three of those, plus the
# channel routes wired to the live gateway. console/ + E2E is Batch C.
#
# This script NEVER runs `git commit`. Review the diff, then commit yourself
# (Gate A) - exactly like every batch before it.
set -euo pipefail

REPO_ROOT="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null || true)"
if [ -z "$REPO_ROOT" ]; then
  echo "error: not inside a git repository. cd into the sharwa_ai repo root first." >&2
  exit 1
fi
if [ ! -f "$REPO_ROOT/docker-compose.yml" ] || [ ! -d "$REPO_ROOT/gateway" ]; then
  echo "error: $REPO_ROOT doesn't look like the sharwa_ai repo root (no docker-compose.yml / gateway/)." >&2
  exit 1
fi
cd "$REPO_ROOT"

if [ -d core ] && [ -n "$(ls -A core 2>/dev/null)" ]; then
  backup="core.bak-$(date +%Y%m%d%H%M%S)"
  echo "core/ already exists and is non-empty - backing it up to $backup/ first (nothing is deleted)."
  cp -r core "$backup"
fi

echo "Extracting core/ ..."
TARBALL_B64=''
echo "$TARBALL_B64" | base64 -d | tar xzf - -C "$REPO_ROOT"

echo
echo "core/ written. File count: $(find core -type f | wc -l)"
echo
cat <<'EOF'
================================================================================
Batch A delivered: core/ backend foundation (code + migrations + its own tests).
Nothing has been started, wired into docker-compose, or committed.

Next steps (manual, on purpose - each is a real decision, not something this
script should guess):

  1) Review the diff:
       git status
       git diff --stat -- core/
       git add -N core/ && git diff -- core/   # shows new files as additions too

  2) Set up the venv (pins in core/requirements.txt - the EXACT versions this
     code was self-verified against; 240/240 real tests, this environment,
     just before delivery):
       cd core
       python3.12 -m venv .venv
       .venv/bin/pip install -r requirements.txt

  3) core/ needs its own env vars (see core/app/config.py's Settings.load()
     for the authoritative list): JWT_ISSUER, JWT_AUDIENCE, and one of
     JWKS_URL / JWT_PUBLIC_KEY_PEM, CORE_DATABASE_URL, CORE_SYSTEM_DATABASE_URL,
     CORE_MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL (a schema-owning role - NOT sharwa_app/
     sharwa_system, which the schema itself denies DDL to), REDIS_CACHE_HOST/
     PORT/PASSWORD, GATEWAY_BASE_URL, GATEWAY_API_KEY, METRICS_TOKEN,
     SSO_LOGIN_URL. These are NEW - this batch deliberately does not touch
     .env or .env.example, since guessing at their real current content here
     would risk a bad edit; Batch B adds them properly once you confirm which
     existing DSNs/roles core/ should reuse.

  4) Apply the migrations for real. Since CORE_MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL almost
     certainly points at a database that ALREADY has this schema (applied by
     hand, long before this migration runner existed), 0001_baseline's SQL
     would collide with the tables it already created if run again verbatim
     - so the FIRST run needs the one-time adoption flag, which records
     0001_baseline as already applied (checksum-verified against the real
     file) WITHOUT re-running its SQL, then applies 0002 normally:
       cd core
       .venv/bin/python -m app.cli migrate --adopt-existing-schema 0001_baseline

     This flag only has any effect while schema_migrations is empty - every
     run after this one (including on a fresh database that never had the
     schema applied by hand) is a plain `app.cli migrate`, no flag needed.
     Before running this, sha256sum core/migrations/0001_baseline.sql against
     whatever created this database's schema (docs/reference/schema.sql, if
     that's still the real source of truth) - the adoption flag trusts you
     that they match; it does not check.

  5) Run the real test suite against your own real infra to double-check
     everything that passed in the sandbox also passes here:
       cd core
       .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/ -v
       .venv/bin/ruff check .
       .venv/bin/lint-imports
       .venv/bin/mypy app/

Batch B (next, no further permission needed): wires the new env vars into
.env.example, adds the `api` service to docker-compose.yml (surgical,
idempotent edit - same pattern as every prior compose change), adds the
channel-lifecycle routes wired to the live gateway, and files the F19/
D-29-equivalent entries into docs/P0_FINDINGS.md and docs/P0_DEVIATIONS.md
against their REAL current content on this VPS.
================================================================================
EOF
